# Vim's own keys cannot be probed — `d`, `w`, `:wq` are the language, not
# bindings, and `vim -es -c map` does not list them. So they are literal here,
# and only the last group, which IS this repo's vimrc, reads from the live file.
name:    vim
what:    the editor that is on every machine. Enough to edit a config and leave
check:   vim
docs:    :help, and vimtutor for a 30-minute guided start

= LEAVING — read this first
Esc              | back to normal mode, where keys are commands not text
jk               | the same thing, without reaching for Esc. From this vimrc
:w               | write the file
:q               | quit
:wq              | write and quit
:q!              | quit and throw away every change
ZZ               | write and quit, no colon

= THE TWO MODES
i                | insert, before the cursor
a                | insert, after the cursor
I                | insert at the start of the line
A                | insert at the end of the line
o                | open a new line below and insert
O                | open a new line above and insert
v                | visual mode — select by moving
V                | visual mode, whole lines
Ctrl-v           | visual mode, a rectangular block

= MOVING
Arrow keys       | work fine. So does the mouse, in this vimrc
w / b            | forward / back one word
0 / $            | start / end of the line
gg / G           | top / bottom of the file
:42              | go to line 42
Ctrl-d / Ctrl-u  | half a screen down / up
{ / }            | previous / next blank line
%                | jump to the matching bracket
5j               | five lines down. The relative numbers exist for this

= EDITING
x                | delete the character under the cursor
dd               | delete the line
dw               | delete to the end of the word
d$               | delete to the end of the line
cw               | change the word — delete it and start inserting
cc               | change the whole line
yy               | copy ("yank") the line
p / P            | paste after / before the cursor
u                | undo
Ctrl-r           | redo
.                | repeat the last change
>> / <<          | indent / outdent the line
J                | join this line with the next

= FINDING AND REPLACING
/text            | search forwards
?text            | search backwards
*                | search for the word under the cursor
:%s/old/new/g    | replace everywhere in the file
:%s/old/new/gc   | the same, confirming each one
:noh             | clear the highlight, the long way

= FILES AND SPLITS
:e path          | open another file
:find name       | open by partial name — this vimrc searches subdirectories
:sp path         | split horizontally
:vs path         | split vertically
:ls              | list open buffers
:b name          | switch to a buffer by partial name
:bd              | close the buffer

= THE CLIPBOARD
y                | with this vimrc, yanks to the system clipboard
p                | pastes from it
"+y              | explicitly the system clipboard, in a stock vim

= WHEN IT WILL NOT LET YOU GO
:q!              | quit, discarding changes
:wq!             | write even if the file is read-only
:w !sudo tee %   | write a root-owned file you opened without sudo
Ctrl-c           | if a command is hanging

= FROM THIS VIMRC
# Read back from the running vim, not from vim/vimrc — so if you change a
# mapping, or drop this repo's vimrc entirely, these rows follow.
probe:   vim-map
conf:    ~/.vimrc
@:w<CR>              | write, without the colon
@:q<CR>              | quit, without the colon
@:nohlsearch<CR>     | clear the search highlight after you have found it
@<C-W>h              | move to the split on the left
@<C-W>j              | the split below
@<C-W>k              | the split above
@<C-W>l              | the split on the right
@nzzzv               | next match, recentred so you can see around it
@Nzzzv               | previous match, recentred
